"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including the preoperative PSA of 8.3 and the pathological T-stage of pT3a with extraprostatic extension. Positive surgical margins are also a noted risk factor. Countervailing findings include no seminal vesicle invasion and an absence of lymphovascular invasion. The biopsy Gleason grade (3+3) differs from the prostatectomy specimen grade (Gleason 3+4). Furthermore, no lymph nodes were removed, meaning nodal status is unknown, which widens uncertainty regarding prognosis.\n\nInformation that would revise this prognosis includes postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory over time. A known follow-up period without an event is documented as the expected follow-up without recurrence. The current data are insufficient to provide a definitive assessment due to missing nodal sampling. The fixed estimated horizon is 56.82 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 36.93–76.70 months; its coverage has not been validated."
